### 2026-08-30 - The inbox reader route, and what it breaks

**What changed:** No code. Investigated why the toolbar click opens the board on
`https://substack.com/inbox/post/213391431`, then amended the Milestone 2A plan.
Task 6 grew from 11 steps to 19: `domain/url.ts` gains `isReaderRoute()`,
`shouldCaptureFrom` gains the route, `substack/extract.ts` gains
`readReaderArticle()` as a TODO(human), and `capture()` prefers that result over
`meta.canonicalUrl` for both url and title. The plan's "Corrections to the design
doc" section gained a fourth correction holding the measurements.
**Why:** Three separate failures, only the first of which is the matcher.
`shouldCaptureFrom` matches `/p/<slug>` and `/pub/<pub>/p/<slug>` and this is
neither. Loosening it alone would have made things worse: the route is an app
shell, so `<head>` describes `/inbox` while `<body>` holds the post, and every
head-first read path in `extract.ts` returns a wrong answer without a null to
signal it. `og:title` is the dangerous one, non-empty and wrong, so the
"couldn't read this page's details" notice never fires. Third, `213391431` is a
Substack row id, not a slug, so `articleKey` falls through to the raw URL and the
same article read twice becomes two cards. Rejected: reading
`link[rel="canonical"]` after hydration, which was the obvious fix and is dead —
measured on the live page, it stays `https://substack.com/inbox`. Rejected:
documenting the route as an exclusion, because the article IS reachable from the
body. Rejected: a separate task ahead of Task 6, which would have meant writing
`capture()` twice.
**Files:** `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-29-milestone-2a.md`,
`docs/learning-notes.md`.
**Verification:** Four DevTools probes against the live route. `.body.markup` is
present and unique and belongs to the open post, so word count, `bodyText`, and
Task 7's quote capture need no change. Walking up from `.body.markup` to the
nearest `/p/` anchor gives the article's own URL, whose link text is the post
title. The unscoped `a[href*="/p/"]` gives 8 links to 8 other publications, the
inbox list still mounted behind the post, so scoping the walk is load-bearing.
No tests run; no code changed.
**State after:** The plan is ready to execute. Nothing in `extension/` moved, so
the route still opens the board.
**Next up:** Unchanged. Task 5 Step 10's browser reload check, then Task 6.
**Open questions:** No reader-route fixture exists in `spike/`. Task 6 Step 9
tests `readReaderArticle` against synthetic HTML, so the unit tests prove the
walk against a model of the page and Step 17 check 6 is what proves the model.
Capturing a real fixture is the fix if that check fails; note that
`spike/README.md` "Risks found" records a signed share token hiding in
`data-attrs` that took three anonymization passes to catch.

### 2026-08-30 - Build the reading side panel (2A Task 5)

**What changed:** Added `cardByArticleKey()` to `db/cards.ts` and its three
tests. Created `src/messages.ts`, the types-only file both the background and
the panel share: `PanelState`, `PANEL_STATE_KEY`, `PanelMessage`,
`CaptureSelectionReply`. Created `src/ui/ReadingPanel.tsx`: it follows the
`PanelState` the background writes on each toolbar click, looks the card up by
article key with `useLiveQuery`, renders it through `CardEditor`, and gives the
footer three status buttons that move the card to the top of a column. Mounted
it at the sidepanel root in place of the Task 1 scaffold. Added the `.reading`
styles.
