### 2026-08-24 - Capture the free article fixture and harden the anonymizer

**What changed:** Captured `spike/fixtures/article-free.html` (100 KB, 1599 body
words) from `escapethecubicle.substack.com`. Added `scrubIdentifiers()`,
`cleanUrl()`, and `cleanEmbedded()` to `spike/capture-fixture.js`, which run as a
new pass 3.5 before `redact()`. Filled the "body word count" and
"paywall / preview state" rows in `spike/README.md` and added five bullets to
"Risks found". Marked Task 3 Steps 1 to 5 complete in the plan.

**Why:** The first three captures leaked the reader's Substack `user_id`
(494698927). `redact()` matches literal strings, so it could not see the id in a
URL path, and could not see it at all inside a base64 share token nested in a
JSON blob in a `data-attrs` attribute. Three leak classes needed three fixes:
delete every query string, zero every 6-or-more digit run in a URL path, and
drop `data-attrs` outright. Dropping `data-attrs` beat parsing its JSON because
nothing downstream reads it: the word count, the button labels, and both button
target URLs all survive the delete, measured.

**Files:** `spike/capture-fixture.js`, `spike/fixtures/article-free.html`,
`spike/README.md`, `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-16-milestone-0.md`,
`docs/learning-notes.md`

**Verification:** `grep` over the fixture returns 0 for the user id, for any JWT,
for `token=`, for `data-attrs`, for the reader's name and email, and for any
query string or 6-digit run in a URL attribute. A linkedom parse confirms
`og:title`, `meta[name=author]`, `link[rel=canonical]`, `og:url`, and both
JSON-LD blocks read correctly, and that `.body.markup` still counts 1599 words,
the same as the unscrubbed capture. `npm test` not run: `extract.js` does not
exist yet.

**State after:** The free fixture is clean and committable. The extractor and its
